sessions.abstract:
The new publication, Stories from Alternate ROOTS: It'llTake some Tellin', represents 50 years of sustained cultural organizing by this legendary, multicultural organization at the intersection of art, activism, and community. The book recounts the struggles and triumphs of the artists and organizations who founded and continue to lead ROOTS. It beautifully captures how a values-driven organization has applied imagination to some of the major challenges of the past half century including racism, poverty, violence, and the eroding of the natural world.&Acirc;

The session consists of three parts. First, one of the book's co-architects, MK Wegman, lays out the the book's organization around ROOTS' mission statement, originally adopted in 1988 and 1996 and alive today:&Acirc; &Acirc;

Alternate ROOTS is an organization based in the Southern USA whose mission is to support the creation and presentation of original art, in all its forms, which is rooted in a particular community of place, tradition, or spirit. As a coalition of cultural workers, we strive to be allies in the elimination of all forms of oppression. ROOTS is committed to social and economic justice and the protection of the natural world and addresses these concerns through its program and services.

Then longtime ROOTer Lisa Mount and ROOTS ally Jan Cohen-Cruz describe their contributions. Lynne Elizabeth, editor of New Village Press, the book's publisher and a longtime friend of IA, points out what is especially unique in the book's conception. Lizzy Cooper Davis, familiar with ROOTS and an IA Board member, then responds to the book, foregrounding what IA stands to learn from ROOTS.

Then we will break into three groups, looking closely at IA's mission and imagining how we could more fully enact it. We will use story circles to that end, a process originally developed by the Free Southern Theater and brought to ROOTS by the iconic playwright/ performer John O'Neal.

Lastly, we'll come back together to discuss how an organization's mission statement can be translated into concrete action. Reflecting the trajectory of a sister organization, what does the book importantly tell us about the role of art and culture in transitions?
